{"name":"Black Sheep","hex":"#0f0d0d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black sheep","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nonconformist; an unusual or unconventional person, who may be rejected or disdained by others for this reason.","A person who is not wholesome, honest, or trustworthy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black_sheep"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Black Sheep (1920 film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Black Sheep is a 1920 British silent romance film directed by Sidney Morgan and starring Marguerite Blanche, George Keene, Eve Balfour, and Arthur Lennard."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Black_Sheep_(1920_film)","wikidata":"Q18708902"},{"title":"Black Sheep (1935 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Black Sheep is a 1935 American mystery drama film directed by Allan Dwan and starring Edmund Lowe, Claire Trevor, and Tom Brown. It was produced by the Fox Film Corporation. The film's sets were designed by the art director Duncan Cramer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/94/Black_Sheep_%281935_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sheep_(1935_film)","wikidata":"Q20715310"},{"title":"Black Sheep (group)","blocks":[{"text":"Black Sheep is an American hip-hop duo from Queens, New York, United States, composed of Andres \"Dres\" Vargas Titus and William \"Mista Lawnge\" McLean. The duo was from New York but met as teenagers in Sanford, North Carolina, where both of their families relocated. The group was part of the Native Tongues collective, which included the Jungle Brothers, De La Soul, and A Tribe Called Quest. After getting together in 1989, Black Sheep debuted in 1991 with the hit song \"Flavor of the Month\" and later released its first album, A Wolf in Sheep's Clothing, which gained them praise and recognition in the hip-hop community for the album's unique rhythms and intelligent, often humorous lyrics. After six years together, Black Sheep disbanded in 1995, citing creative differences."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b7/Black_Sheep.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Sheep_(group)","wikidata":"Q258120"},{"title":"Black sheep","blocks":[{"text":"In the English language, black sheep is an idiom that describes a member of a group who is different from the rest, especially a family member who does not fit in. The term stems from sheep whose fleece is colored black rather than the more common white; these sheep stand out in the flock, and their wool is worth less because it cannot be dyed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Black_sheep-1.jpg/330px-Black_sheep-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_sheep","wikidata":"Q606128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg/500px-The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg/1280px-The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg/500px-The_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Black Sheep from a 1901 edition of Mother Goose by William Wallace Denslow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Black_sheep_illustrated_by_William_Wallace_Denslow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg/500px-Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg/1280px-Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg/500px-Miros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_%281987%2901.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black Pope and Black Sheep, a sculpture by Mirosław Bałka, 1987","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiros%C5%82aw_Ba%C5%82ka_-_Czarny_papie%C5%BC_i_czarna_owca_(1987)01.jpg"}],"sounds":{"black sheep":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/En-au-black_sheep.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/2e/En-au-black_sheep.ogg/En-au-black_sheep.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Black hole - Messier 87","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Black_hole_-_Messier_87.jpg/500px-Black_hole_-_Messier_87.jpg","ratio":1.7166666666666666,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87.jpg","share":0.78125},{"title":"Black hole - Messier 87 crop max res","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg/500px-Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg","ratio":1,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg","sha